SOURCES={2022:'https://github.com/frc604/2022-public',2023:'https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/team-604-quixilver-presents-our-2023-robot-and-code-release/447255',2024:'https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/team-604-quixilver-presents-our-2024-robot-cad-and-code-release/477799',2025:'https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/team-604-quixilver-cad-code-release-for-our-2025-robot-the-flush/502824',2026:'https://www.chiefdelphi.com/t/team-604-quixilver-2026-robot-cad-and-code-release/524289'}
# subsystem, configuration, construction, stated rationale, interpretation
NOTES={
2022:[
('drivetrain','Published FRC-2022 code','Calibration defines a 20.5-inch wheelbase and track width, 3.94-inch wheel diameter, 6.75:1 drive reduction and 12.8:1 steering reduction. These are software configuration values, not independent measurements.','Mechanical selection rationale is not established by the code.','The swerve and localization must be considered together. A configured maximum speed is not a demonstrated match speed. Wheel calibration affects both the path tracker and the launch-distance estimate, so a copied drive configuration can corrupt scoring even when teleop feels acceptable. Check hardware revision, encoder scales and actual loaded wheel circumference before borrowing constants.'),
('intake','Published FRC-2022 code','Intake.java configures distinct Falcon 500 roller and deployment motors, a trapezoidal deployment profile, arm feedforward and a 45A deployment stator limit. Intake, eject, unjam and yeet commands are separate entry points.','The code does not state why this exact mechanical geometry was selected.','The published interface treats recovery and reverse flow as designed operating modes. That is useful architectural evidence, but it does not establish roller material, width, compression or impact survival. A profiled pivot can reduce abrupt actuation; whether it does so under contact depends on the real mechanism and controller settings. Preserve these unknowns rather than infer a later 604 polycarbonate intake in 2022.'),
('transfer','Published FRC-2022 code','BallPath.java separates upper and lower Falcon-driven transport sections and exposes position/velocity control and sensor-based ball handling.','Exact transport geometry and team selection rationale remain unresolved.','Separately controlled transport can stage a second cargo while releasing the first, but introduces coordination between occupancy sensing, launcher readiness and reverse recovery. Code supports the existence of these interfaces, not an independent throughput result or proof of jam-free behavior. A two-cargo game rewards preserving a reliable staged sequence; extra transport complexity should be justified by actual scoring continuity.'),
('scoring','Published FRC-2022 code','Launcher.java configures front and rear Falcon flywheel motors with feedforward and feedback. Readiness checks use a five-sample speed buffer and tolerance; distance-related bounds are treated separately.','The source establishes readiness logic, not a published mechanical reason for wheel geometry.','Requiring recent samples within tolerance is different from checking one instantaneous RPM. It can reject a momentary speed crossing after spin-up or a previous shot. The trade is delayed release versus launch consistency. The existence of front/rear motors does not establish independent spin control in competition, wheel dimensions, a turret, or exact release angle. Those require geometry and event evidence.'),
('endgame','Published FRC-2022 code','Climber.java configures three NEO motors and staged first-rung, lift, handoff, high-rung and traversal transitions. It uses filtered tilt and tilt rate, position/profile completion and an angle-history lockout.','Comments explicitly describe gyro-controlled transitions and an antenna-touch-angle lockout; broader hardware intent is not fully documented here.','This is a dynamic climb sequence, not a simple winch toggle. A transition may require both operator approval and a suitable swing/position state. That ties compliance, sensor filtering, angular thresholds and operator timing together. A traversal state in released code proves intended capability, not a successful traversal in every match or that all events ran this revision. Exact hook and support geometry remain open.'),
('controls','Original code versus learning rewrite','The repository README identifies FRC-2022 as robot code and FRC-2022-NEW as a work-in-progress reimplementation for learning and cleanup. Quikplan-swerve and the offboard quixpf particle filter are separate directories.','The rewrite is explicitly described as preparation and learning.','Do not read the cleaner NEW tree and retroactively claim every behavior was fielded in 2022. The original tree is the stronger starting point for event-era behavior, though its final commit still lacks per-event installation certification. Pin the repository commit and file path when citing code; preserve the distinction between software configuration, mechanical construction and observed performance.')],
2023:[
('intake','2023 published robot account','A pasta-roller intake grips a cone flange and pinches a cube, constraining cones to tip-in or tip-out. A roller reorienter supplies the additional rotational degree of freedom needed for an upright handoff. Rollers are powered coaxially through the reorienter.','The team wanted ground pickup in arbitrary orientations; human-player throwing could shorten travel by an estimated 40% and reduce loading-zone traffic. Coaxial drive protects motors and reduces mass at the intake edge.','This is an explicit strategy-to-mechanism chain: changing the pickup source reduces travel only if orientation and handoff remain reliable. The 40% is the team’s route estimate, not an independently measured cycle improvement. A broad capture envelope transfers complexity downstream unless the robot can reduce the resulting pose uncertainty. Compare full acquisition-to-scoring cycles, including difficult orientations, rather than pickup speed alone.'),
('transfer','2023 published robot account','A linear intake slider brings the piece inward and permits frontal compliance. A separate horizontal scoring-arm slider reaches across the intake. A time-of-flight sensor first distinguishes orientation at a fixed location, then locates the piece laterally. The elevator lowers while the intake expels to hand off.','The team needed handoff despite variable piece position across the intake width.','Do not collapse the two sliders into a single motion. The intake reduces some uncertainty, then sensing and the receiving mechanism handle what remains. This avoids requiring perfect passive centering, at the cost of coordinated motion, sensor interpretation and collision clearance. Sensor occlusion, flange shape, cone reflectivity and loaded mechanism deflection are plausible validation concerns, not failures established by this publication.'),
('scoring','2023 published robot account','A non-parallel four-bar provides scoring reach. An Everybot-style lightweight gripper squeezes the cone tip and pinches a cube. The release describes scoring all levels.','The team chose the linkage for low mass and long extension, and judged the high-versus-middle scoring difference sufficient to justify the added capability.','The linkage choice belongs with the piece acquisition and handoff design. Saving moving mass can reduce the drive and tipping burden, but linkage workspace, joint play and gripper compliance determine the usable placement envelope. A later team using a single-stage elevator should not be read as proof that this four-bar was a failed architecture; the game, interface and required motion differ.'),
('controls','2023 published robot account','Originally the operator selected scoring locations on a driverstation keyboard and the driver triggered automated scoring. The team later reports the driver becoming faster and more consistent than that sequence, reducing the operator role to piece and level selection.','The control allocation evolved with driver performance.','Automation should be judged against practiced human operation, including recovery, rather than valued simply because it exists. This is a useful counterexample to treating 604’s later single-driver automation as an unchanging philosophy implemented identically every year. Keep the final software release and its 2024-beta port distinct. Detailed drivetrain specifications and the charge-station balancing mechanism are not established by this reviewed release.')],
2024:[
('intake','2024 release and May 2025 follow-up','Polycarbonate rollers flex under impact; short dead-axle sections avoid a full-width shaft permanently bending. The team reports rollers deflecting more than half their diameter in bad hits. Deployment ties the two sides through chain and a cross shaft inside the frame.','Compliance, weight and packaging drove the split-axle construction. The team explicitly reports axle-mount bolts shearing and deployment chain snapping, unresolved during the season.','A compliant roller is not the same as a robust entire intake. Moving deformation away from a long shaft can concentrate load in axle bolts or a mechanically coupled deployment system. This matters directly to 1833’s request for something beefy: copy the load path and its known limitations, not merely the visible material. The 2026 independently driven arms are a later response to the chain failure, not a configuration that existed throughout 2024.'),
('controls','2024 published CAD versus installed hardware','The team says bellypan holes were hand-drilled and omitted from CAD, electronics were suppressed to reduce CAD lag, shooter shafts were actually cut shorter, and a reused climber shaft tensioned surgical tubing absent from CAD.','Some differences were drafting omissions, some deliberate reuse and some effort-versus-benefit decisions.','A released CAD assembly is a valuable geometry reference but not a perfect as-built record. A long shaft in a rendering is not automatically an intended design feature. Likewise, suppressed electronics do not imply an unplanned electrical layout. Follow the team’s replies when interpreting the release. The existing interview synopsis documents the asymmetric launcher and trap system; this addition focuses on the explicitly published manufacturing and intake corrections.')],
2025:[
('drivetrain','The Flush in-season release','29-inch square chassis with WCP X2i modules and a 5.9:1 drive ratio; the team optimized for roughly 18-foot average sprint distance. Protective panels isolate electronics from falling coral.','Stability at high reach and the expected travel distance drive the stated choices.','Optimize acceleration and usable travel under the expected route distribution, rather than unloaded top speed alone. A larger support polygon can help a reaching superstructure while increasing congestion exposure. The published dimensions and ratio are a configuration snapshot; they do not prove the same ratio is best for 1833 or a different game.'),
('scoring','The Flush elevator and arm','Single-stage elevator uses 2x2 uprights, cross bracing and chain drive on both sides. The release gives two Kraken X60s, 3:1 and 28 inches of motion; stator limits increased from 40A to 80A. The arm is rigidly tied side-to-side and its gearbox shimmed; the team describes detuning and reducing the drive to one motor at 100A to avoid tipping.','Rigidity, low backlash and precise automated control are explicit priorities.','Motor count and theoretical joint acceleration cannot be separated from robot stability. Dual-sided chain drive addresses carriage twist, while a rigid camera mount helps relate vision measurements to mechanism geometry. Preserve the stated revisions and the release’s mixed original/final motor annotations; do not silently combine every number into one certified event configuration.'),
('intake','The Flush station funnel and gripper','A full-width 4mm corrugated-plastic funnel loads from three sides through separately powered rollers. The gripper has independently powered left/right rollers, four coral rollers and two algae rollers, plus an open top.','The team wanted tolerant loading, horizontal L1 packing and precise placement. The open top avoids reef entanglement and permits a shallower coral release angle.','A funnel can absorb human-player variability while the gripper controls the final release. Independent sides support a different L1 placement policy rather than merely increasing raw roller power. This is an architectural option for a station-fed role, not evidence that floor coral pickup was unnecessary for every alliance. Corrugated plastic stiffness, folding and clearances must be evaluated in the installed package.'),
('endgame','The Flush climb/funnel interface','The release describes a roughly 3.5-pound climber including motor and gearbox, stowed below the funnel, surgical-tubing extension and a 15:1 winch with a 2:1 pulley. A rear funnel ramp unfolds to clear the cage and climb.','Compact packaging preserves the loading system while providing cage engagement tolerance.','The climb is coupled to the funnel’s motion and the loaded robot CG. A compact mechanism is not necessarily easy to approach under traffic; validate capture, retraction and failures with the actual funnel and piece state. These are team-reported design quantities, not measured masses or an exhaustive installed-version history.'),
('controls','The Flush and separate Quixshot offseason machine','The Flush uses predictive target selection, smooth handover from driver motion, reef-clearance sequencing and separate local/global localization. The team reports 547/549 L2-L4 coral placements over its last two events. Quixshot is a distinct offseason coral launcher with manually tuned launch velocity differentials and a one-time intake deploy latch.','The team reports that packaging a ground pickup with the in-season climber became difficult, so the compatible pickup was instead installed on the offseason launcher.','Do not attach Quixshot’s launcher to the in-season elevator robot. The 99.6% is a bounded team-reported placement statistic, not full-match reliability or 99.6% for every task. The published offseason answer says it did not deep-climb or handle algae at that point. The strongest transferable lesson is a deliberate interface and packaging trade, not the assertion that launching always beats placing.')],
2026:[
('transfer','Toploader DPC serializer','A rotating capture mechanism scoops fuel from a circular plywood bucket. Rotation and up-feed have separate motors; side rollers use omni wheels. Successive fuel pushes through a corrugated-plastic tube shaped by printed ends. The bucket separates into two sections for service.','The team identified serialization as the limiting process and designed the robot around it. Independent drives allow software adjustment of rotation versus up-feed.','The capture point moves to available fuel rather than relying solely on gravity bringing fuel to a stationary throat. Independent feed authority can help tune pile interaction and column delivery separately, but the release does not establish a universal motor-ratio setting. Bucket geometry, turret support and electronics access are part of the same flow system. Prototype rates of about 15 BPS single-stream and 23 BPS full-width are comparison claims, not sustained match scoring rates.'),
('drivetrain','Toploader chassis','A 27-inch square frame preserves circular bucket area without custom swerves or chamfered corners. Internal bars protect battery and module compartments; rear bumper mounts integrate armor for serializer protection.','Maximize the serializer’s available area with manufacturable hardware and protect internal mechanisms.','This is an explicit packaging choice. It sacrifices some geometric freedom to keep the central circular feed volume useful with standard drive components. Frame perimeter, usable interior area and turret swept volume must be compared together. The team separately describes bump-only autonomous constraints; no unreviewed trench state or chassis specification should be inferred.'),
('scoring','Toploader inset turret','The turret is supported from above and packaged to reduce swept volume. Its sliding bearing stacks polycarbonate and aluminum plates; printed PETG spacers set clearance empirically and grease reduces friction.','Moving scoring and feeding were judged worth a lower single-stream ceiling; small swept volume preserves hopper capacity and flow.','A turret changes more than aiming. Its supports and swept envelope can obstruct fuel entering the serializer, so a nominal capacity comparison that ignores the moving assembly is incomplete. This release supports a manufacturable impact-tolerant bearing concept, not exact dimensions, a universal bearing preload or independently verified shot accuracy. The team’s rate trade is tied to its intended moving role.'),
('intake','Toploader independently deployed arms','Each intake arm uses an X44 deploy motor. Protected motors belt-drive compliant dead-axle polycarbonate rollers with silicone tubing and TPU pulley plugs. TPU belt supports retain the belts during deflection. A one-time kicker deploy uses surgical tubing and flexible plugs/string.','The team explicitly connects independent deployment to eliminating the mechanically linked 2024 chain failure. Polycarbonate was preferred over SRPP to avoid permanent deformation changing belt centers.','Compliance must preserve transmission function, not just prevent fracture. A flexible arm changes belt geometry under load; supporting the belt during that motion is an integral part of the architecture. The team reports that TPU replaced brittle PETG roller plugs, with 100% infill and four-wall printing, and that wear still requires replacement every one or two events. Repairable consumables are not maintenance-free hardware.'),
('intake','Toploader linked sliding hopper','Intake deployment extends a sliding hopper; surgical tubing retracts it. Two laminated layers of corrugated plastic provide stiffness. Slots are reinforced with 0.090-inch polycarbonate sandwiched between sheets; panels use tape and adhesive for replacement.','The publication emphasizes low mass, robust sliding and easy manufacture/repair with limited router access.','This is a specific example of an intake–storage interface, useful to 1833’s morph chart. Linking deployment reduces independent actuation but also links faults and operating states. Slot reinforcement addresses local bearing and deformation loads that a generic corrugated-plastic wall description misses. Capacity, deflection and passage access should be measured in each loaded state; no unverified hopper capacity is assigned here.'),
('controls','Toploader readiness and localization','Scoring requires launch velocity, launch angle and turret yaw within tolerance plus a recent image with at least two tags. The serializer pauses when readiness fails. PhotonVision MultiTag selects camera poses using hub/total tag visibility and rejects old or ambiguous poses.','Single-driver operation reduces communication overhead; automated gating is intended to release fuel only from useful launch states.','Feed interruption is an intentional interface between aiming and serialization, not automatically a jam. A high mechanical BPS ceiling is useful only while readiness remains true. Logging the reason for each gating interruption would distinguish vision starvation, aim error and actual feed trouble. The release does not demonstrate those duty fractions or an electrical diagnosis of every stop.'),
('controls','Toploader partner-aware bump autonomous','The team describes timestamping partner paths, targeting leftover fuel after the trench race, using reusable path partials and adjustable pauses without redeployment. A Pac-Man routine is identified at NorCal DCMP playoff match 1.','A bump-only robot was expected to reach midfield later; route selection was built around shorter partners rather than competing for the same initial race.','Architectural limitations can become planning inputs instead of being hidden by an ideal autonomous animation. Preserve the team’s distinction between pre-Champs crossing rules and later rules, and between experiments and actually used routines. A clever path is not proof every prototype routine competed. The release does not establish a tower mechanism; absence from the selected technical sections remains unknown, not certified absence.')]
}
